{"name":"Karma Chameleon","hex":"#9f78a9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"karma","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The sum total of a person's actions, which determine the person's next incarnation in samsara, the cycle of death and rebirth.","A force or law of nature which causes one to reap what one sows; destiny; fate."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/karma"},{"term":"chameleon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small to mid-size reptile, of the family Chamaeleonidae, and one of the best known lizard families able to change color and project its long tongue.","A person with inconstant behavior; one able to quickly adjust to new circumstances."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["That changes or modifies its color."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chameleon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Karma Chameleon","blocks":[{"text":"\"Karma Chameleon\" is a song by English band Culture Club, featured on the group's 1983 album Colour by Numbers. The single was released in the United Kingdom in September 1983 and became the second Culture Club single to reach the top of the UK singles chart, after \"Do You Really Want to Hurt Me\". The record stayed at number one for six weeks and became the UK's biggest-selling single of the year 1983, selling 955,000 copies in 1983 and certificated platinum by BPI. To date, it is the 38th-biggest-selling single of all time in the UK, selling over 1.52 million copies."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/a8/Nw171.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karma_Chameleon","wikidata":"Q917304"},{"title":"Karma","blocks":[{"text":"Karma is an ancient Indian concept that refers to an action, work, or deed, and its effect or consequences. In Indian religions, the term more specifically refers to a principle of cause and effect, often descriptively called the principle of karma, wherein individuals' intent and actions (cause) influence their future (effect): Good intent and good deeds contribute to good karma and happier rebirths, while bad intent and bad deeds contribute to bad karma and worse rebirths. In some scriptures, however, there is no link between rebirth and karma."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/EndlessKnot03d.png/330px-EndlessKnot03d.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karma","wikidata":"Q132196"},{"title":"Chameleon","blocks":[{"text":"Chameleons are a distinctive and highly specialized clade of Old World lizards with 200 species described as of June 2015. The members of this family are best known for their distinct range of colours, being capable of colour-shifting camouflage. The large number of species in the family exhibit considerable variability in their capacity to change colour. For some, it is more of a shift of brightness ; for others, a plethora of colour-combinations can be seen."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Panther_Chameleon_738367_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Panther_Chameleon_738367_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chameleon","wikidata":"Q37686"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/New_Southern_Belle_%283671473221%29.jpg/500px-New_Southern_Belle_%283671473221%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/New_Southern_Belle_%283671473221%29.jpg/500px-New_Southern_Belle_%283671473221%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The New Southern Belle, the Thames riverboat used in video[20]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANew_Southern_Belle_(3671473221).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Hand_made_prayer_wheel_from_Nepal.jpg/500px-Hand_made_prayer_wheel_from_Nepal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Hand_made_prayer_wheel_from_Nepal.jpg/1280px-Hand_made_prayer_wheel_from_Nepal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Hand_made_prayer_wheel_from_Nepal.jpg/500px-Hand_made_prayer_wheel_from_Nepal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHand_made_prayer_wheel_from_Nepal.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Karma_AS.jpg/330px-Karma_AS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Karma_AS.jpg/330px-Karma_AS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Karma as action and reaction: if we show goodness, we will reap goodness","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKarma_AS.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Tema_Nezahat_Gokyigit_Park_1060584_nymphaea.jpg/500px-Tema_Nezahat_Gokyigit_Park_1060584_nymphaea.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Tema_Nezahat_Gokyigit_Park_1060584_nymphaea.jpg/1280px-Tema_Nezahat_Gokyigit_Park_1060584_nymphaea.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Tema_Nezahat_Gokyigit_Park_1060584_nymphaea.jpg/500px-Tema_Nezahat_Gokyigit_Park_1060584_nymphaea.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Lotus symbolically represents karma in many Asian traditions. A blooming lotus flower is one of the few flowers that simultaneously carries seeds inside itself while it blooms. Seed is symbolically seen as cause, the flower effect. Lotus is also considered as a reminder that one can grow, share good karma and remain unstained even in muddy circumstances[46]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATema_Nezahat_Gokyigit_Park_1060584_nymphaea.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Types_of_Karma.JPG/500px-Types_of_Karma.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Types_of_Karma.JPG/500px-Types_of_Karma.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Types of Karmas as per Jain philosophy","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATypes_of_Karma.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Thirthankara_Suparshvanath_Museum_Rietberg_RVI_306.jpg/500px-Thirthankara_Suparshvanath_Museum_Rietberg_RVI_306.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Thirthankara_Suparshvanath_Museum_Rietberg_RVI_306.jpg/1280px-Thirthankara_Suparshvanath_Museum_Rietberg_RVI_306.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Thirthankara_Suparshvanath_Museum_Rietberg_RVI_306.jpg/500px-Thirthankara_Suparshvanath_Museum_Rietberg_RVI_306.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Shrivatsa or the karmic knot depicted on the chest of the Tirthankara","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThirthankara_Suparshvanath_Museum_Rietberg_RVI_306.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/It_Shoots_Further_Than_He_Dreams.jpg/500px-It_Shoots_Further_Than_He_Dreams.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_